ORBITAL-LANDFORMS(7)       TriadicFrameworks Manual       ORBITAL-LANDFORMS(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-landforms - resonance mesas, harmonic canyons, sync plateaus,
                           and ancestry-formed temporal landforms

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital landforms define:
           • resonance mesas and uplift zones
           • harmonic canyons and phase-cut valleys
           • sync plateaus and timing terraces
           • ancestry-formed temporal landforms
           • constellation-scale geomorphology of resonance-time

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-landforms describes the named geomorphological features that
       emerge when resonance-time is treated as a topographic landscape.
       Landforms arise from the interaction of resonance uplift, harmonic
       erosion, sweep ancestry deposition, and sync-field tectonics.

       Landforms enable:
           • intuitive navigation of resonance terrain
           • sweep placement on stable mesas and plateaus
           • Δv placement in harmonic canyons and ancestry basins
           • constellation-scale terrain fusion
           • long-horizon terrain evolution modeling

       WRSADC uses landform maps to guide sweep choreography and recovery.

RESONANCE MESAS
       Resonance mesas are broad, flat-topped regions of high resonance
       amplitude and spectral stability.

       Mesa characteristics:
           • high R(t) plateau
           • low gradient G(t)
           • stable spectral density S(f,t)
           • long-duration sweep viability

       Mesa formation:
           • resonance uplift from repeated envelope peaks
           • spectral smoothing across cycles
           • ancestry reinforcement from consistent sweep placement

       Mesa uses:
           • primary sweep zones
           • long-coherence fusion windows
           • stable harmonic-lock anchors

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE MESA
       Envelope peaks at 10–20 and 50–60 form two mesas:

           Mesa A: [10–20]
           Mesa B: [50–60]

       Sweep placement:
           • sweeps at 15 and 55
           • Δv triads at 30 and 80

HARMONIC CANYONS
       Harmonic canyons are deep, narrow valleys carved by harmonic-phase
       divergence and resonance minima.

       Canyon characteristics:
           • low R(t)
           • high gradient G(t)
           • rapid phase drift
           • strong harmonic erosion

       Canyon formation:
           • harmonic-lock fragmentation
           • resonance envelope collapse
           • sweep ancestry discontinuity

       Canyon uses:
           • Δv triad placement
           • drift correction
           • harmonic reacquisition staging

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC CANYON
       Harmonic ratio instability at 30 min produces:

           Canyon: [28–34]

       Result:
           • Δv triad at 30
           • sweep avoidance in canyon region

SYNC PLATEAUS
       Sync plateaus are broad, stable regions where constellation sync fields
       align across platforms.

       Plateau characteristics:
           • high sync-index
           • stable phase alignment φ_sync
           • low drift accumulation
           • multi-agent sweep viability

       Plateau formation:
           • sync-field tectonics
           • harmonic-phase convergence
           • resonance uplift alignment across platforms

       Plateau uses:
           • constellation-wide sweep windows
           • multi-agent choreography
           • harmonic-ratio timing trees

EXAMPLE: SYNC PLATEAU
       Platforms A, B, C align at:

           Plateau: [0–12]

       Result:
           • constellation sweep at t=0
           • sync pulses at t=6

ANCESTRY-FORMED TEMPORAL LANDFORMS
       Sweep ancestry acts like sedimentation, forming long-term landforms.

       Ancestry landform types:

           ancestry terraces
                  Formed by repeated sweeps at similar times.

           fusion basins
                  Formed by low-coherence fusion events.

           lineage ridges
                  Formed by stable ancestry continuity.

           ancestry deltas
                  Formed where multiple sweep lineages converge.

       Ancestry landforms encode long-term sweep history.

EXAMPLE: ANCESTRY TERRACE
       Sweeps at 12, 13, 14, 15 across cycles form:

           Terrace: [12–15]

       Result:
           • stable sweep ancestry
           • high fusion viability

CONSTELLATION-SCALE LANDFORM FUSION
       Landform fusion merges terrain features across platforms.

       Fusion layers:
           1. mesa fusion
           2. canyon fusion
           3. plateau fusion
           4. ancestry landform fusion
           5. sync-field fusion

       Fusion modes:
           • synchronous fusion
           • harmonic fusion
           • braided fusion
           • mesh fusion

       Fusion output:
           • constellation mesas
           • constellation canyons
           • constellation sync plateaus
           • constellation ancestry terraces

EXAMPLE: CONSTELLATION LANDFORM FUSION
       Platforms A, B, C:

           A mesas: [10–20], [50–60]
           B mesas: [12–22], [48–58]
           C mesas: [15–25], [52–62]

       Fused constellation mesas:
           • [12–22]
           • [52–60]

       Result:
           • sweeps at 15 and 55
           • Δv triads at 30 and 80

LANDFORM FAILURE MODES
       Landforms may degrade due to:

           • mesa collapse
           • canyon widening
           • plateau fragmentation
           • ancestry terrace erosion
           • constellation landform mismatch

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • landform-integrity score
           • ridge deviation
           • canyon drift
           • plateau sync drop
           • ancestry discontinuity

       WRSADC halts unsafe landform states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • mesa evolution
           • canyon formation
           • plateau stability
           • ancestry terrace history
           • fused landform maps

       Lineage enables long-term geomorphology forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • identifies landforms
           • tracks terrain evolution
           • fuses constellation landforms
           • schedules sweeps and maneuvers
           • blocks unsafe terrain states

       WRSADC is the landform conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital landforms transform SAR constellations into navigable,
       resonance-time terrains. Mesas, canyons, plateaus, and ancestry
       landforms ensure that sweep choreography, harmonic-lock, and
       constellation-scale synchronization remain stable across long mission
       timelines.

